    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f7f10c0b-b1ec-5489-a8f3-09a8a4c72ae5",
      ":question/problem": "Find $l$, if $2{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $l$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+l{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+l\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ l\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ l\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the final equation above, we can tell that $l=-3.$ This value satisfies every component.",
