    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ac4ce13d-59cc-5c86-afcf-0531eb30877e",
      ":question/problem": "Find $m$, if $3{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $m$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+m\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ m\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the second components, we get\n\n$$\n-6m=18\\,⟹\\,m=-3.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $m$ such that $3{a}_{1}+m{a}_{2}=0.$",
